INT. LAUNDROMAT, 24-HOUR - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent hum. Rows of dryers tumbling. A vending machine glows. The windows are black.

A TALL MAN in a work jacket sits on a folding table, a duffel bag at his feet. He stares at the dryers. Across the room, VERONICA CASTELLANOS, thirty, scrubs, sits with a laundry basket in her lap, scrolling her phone. At the folding table's far end, a younger man, NIKOLAI BRODSKY, hoodie up, folds towels very slowly. And behind the counter, DOLORES, sixty, the attendant, reads a paperback.

TALL MAN
Excuse me. Is this machine broken?

VERONICA
It says out of order.

TALL MAN
It took my quarters.

DOLORES
There's a number on the sign.

TALL MAN
Is the number answered?

DOLORES
Not at three.

The man stands. He is very large. He crosses to the dryer and lays a palm against its door. Nikolai's eyes rise from the towels.

NIKOLAI
I'd leave it alone.

TALL MAN
Who asked you?

NIKOLAI
Nobody. Just saying.

TALL MAN
What's in the dryer, kid?

NIKOLAI
Nothing.

TALL MAN
Then why are you worried?

Veronica sets down her phone. Dolores sets down her book.

VERONICA
Sir, you should sit down.

TALL MAN
Sit down yourself.

He yanks the door. A canvas satchel tumbles out, hits the floor, and spills bundled cash. Nikolai lunges. The tall man shoves him back into the folding table, and towels burst across the floor.

MARCUS
(holding up a badge)
Police. Everybody stay where you are.

Veronica and Nikolai freeze. Dolores slowly raises her hands. Marcus kneels over the satchel, his badge dangling from his chain.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Who owns this?

DOLORES
He does.

NIKOLAI
She's lying.

VERONICA
He hid it this afternoon. I saw him.

NIKOLAI
She was in on it!

Marcus stands. He looks at the three of them, a long beat. He takes out his cuffs.
